Largest Command Complete for every Broadcom vendor command (OGF 0x3f), not only 0xfdf0.
With 1132, bluetoothd (iOS 26) still aborted at each start, now in _BCM_LowPowerCoreStatsEventParser
("ByteStream_NumReadBytesAvail(*pBs) >= 4", bcm_vsc.c:1017-1021) after a run of vendor commands iOS 18 never sent
(0xfe2c, 0xfce9, 0xfe37, 0xfe29, 0xfe3b). bcm_vsc.c's reply parsers only check for enough bytes, so the sub-opcode
followed by 250 zeros reads as zero statistics; standard commands keep the 16-byte reply.

diff --git a/bsd/dev/beryllium_bt_hci.c b/bsd/dev/beryllium_bt_hci.c
--- a/bsd/dev/beryllium_bt_hci.c
+++ b/bsd/dev/beryllium_bt_hci.c
@@ -158,10 +158,16 @@
  * for by PerfPowerServices right after the HCI init) with 16 bytes tripped "ByteStream_NumReadBytesAvail(*pB...)" in
  * bcm_vsc.c:196 and bluetoothd aborted on every start (iOS 26, 2026-10-29); zero statistics are harmless.
  */
+/*
+ * 1133: every vendor command (OGF 0x3f) gets the largest reply. iOS 26's bluetoothd has more statistics parsers than the
+ * one 1132 met: after 1132 it aborted in _BCM_LowPowerCoreStatsEventParser ("ByteStream_NumReadBytesAvail(*pBs) >= 4",
+ * bcm_vsc.c:1017-1021) at the end of its vendor-command run. bcm_vsc.c's parsers only check that enough bytes are left,
+ * so zeros after the sub-opcode read as zero statistics or zero entries. Standard commands keep 16 bytes.
+ */
 static unsigned
 bt_compat_pad(unsigned op)
 {
-	return op == 0xfdf0 ? BT_COMPAT_PAD_MAX : BT_COMPAT_PAD;
+	return (op >> 10) == 0x3f ? BT_COMPAT_PAD_MAX : BT_COMPAT_PAD;
 }
 
 /* First parameter byte of the last few commands sent, by opcode (for rewritten replies). */
